# Copy to ~/.mayo-watchdog.env ON THE MINI, fill in, chmod 600.
# NEVER commit the real file — it is outside the repo on purpose.
#
# Getting the two values:
#   1. Talk to @BotFather on Telegram -> /newbot -> it gives you the token.
#   2. Send any message to your new bot, then:
#      curl -s "https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates" | grep -o '"id":[0-9-]*'
#      The chat id is the one in the "chat" object (negative for groups).

TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=<from @BotFather>
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=<your chat id>

# --- optional overrides ---------------------------------------------------
# Alert above this % used on /
# DISK_PCT_MAX=85
# Alert below this % system-wide free memory
# MEM_FREE_PCT_MIN=15
# Alert above this % of a container's own memory limit
# CONTAINER_MEM_PCT_MAX=90
# While a check stays broken, re-notify at most this often (hours)
# REPEAT_HOURS=6
# Where the ELK stack lives (for the Elasticsearch health check)
# ELK_DIR=$HOME/programs/work/creiip/claude-code-space/infra/elk

# --- Elasticsearch ---------------------------------------------------------
# Read-only credentials for the heap check. Elasticsearch is judged on HEAP,
# not on container memory: a JVM claims its container's limit and keeps it, so
# resident size sat at 90-97% while the heap was half used and the cluster held
# a few MB of data. Heap exhaustion is the thing that precedes an OOM.
# Same values as infra/elk/.env on the host — never commit them.
ELASTIC_USER=elastic
ELASTIC_PASSWORD=<same as infra/elk/.env>
ELASTIC_PORT=9200
# Fires at 85 rather than 90: above ~85% the GC starts spending real time on
# full collections, so this is meant to warn while there is still room to act.
ES_HEAP_PCT_MAX=85
